; regression test for the most severe soundness bug found this session: `load logic,
; equality` alone (nothing else -- no set.kurt, no arith.kurt, unrelated to any specific
; theory) let ANY two constants be proven equal, with no premise connecting them at all.
; Confirmed the minimal reproduction directly before fixing it -- this exact file, unchanged,
; used to print `Proof checked.` instead of failing.
;
; root cause: applying forall-elim (`(forall $x %A) implies (sub $x $a %A)`) to prove `f = g`
; can decompose the goal via the "whole expression is the hole" single-hole decomposition,
; giving `%A := ($x = g)`, `$a := f`. forall-elim's own premise (`forall $x %A`) then needs
; `%A`, i.e. `$x = g`, to hold -- for a `$x` that's supposed to be a fresh eigenvariable
; standing for "any value at all" (that's the whole point of forall-elim's own antecedent).
; But nothing prevented `$x` itself from being unified straight to the concrete value `g`
; during that premise search (matching against "equal-intro", $a=$a) -- letting a variable
; that must mean "true no matter what x is" instead mean "true for this one x we picked",
; which defeats the entire soundness of universal generalization. See doc/kurt-soundness.md's
; dedicated writeup for the full mechanism and the fix (blocking that eigenvariable from being
; assigned during the premise search).
load logic, equality
const f, g
expect "ProofError"
    f = g
;;; Proof checked.
